\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The two mechanisms on the Rainier capture, both top-down over the \SI {200}{\meter } grid. Population synthesis (a) extends the observed tree detections outward across the region where that class is admissible, matching their measured spatial statistics rather than repeating a fixed density. Ground cover (b) is a mask, not a set of instances: it marks where the scattered tuft geometry is admissible. The mask is small relative to the grid because it is derived from the region typing of the \emph  {original} panorama, which only sees meadow in the near field the photograph actually observed.}}{17}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rainier-populations}{{9}{17}{The two mechanisms on the Rainier capture, both top-down over the \SI {200}{\meter } grid. Population synthesis (a) extends the observed tree detections outward across the region where that class is admissible, matching their measured spatial statistics rather than repeating a fixed density. Ground cover (b) is a mask, not a set of instances: it marks where the scattered tuft geometry is admissible. The mask is small relative to the grid because it is derived from the region typing of the \emph {original} panorama, which only sees meadow in the near field the photograph actually observed}{figure.caption.15}{}}
\newlabel{fig:rainier-populations@cref}{{[figure][9][]9}{[1][16][]17}{}{}{}}
